# Sorting the post: a model reads each letter, and the program decides.
#
# The judging is done by a model built to answer closed questions with how
# likely each answer is, in the manner of TypeSafe's Jev. A model that writes
# text gives each letter a one-line summary. Everything that is decided is
# decided here, in ordinary sentences: one question at a time, and three bands
# for how sure the answer is, as Jev's documentation recommends.
#
# Run it as it is and no model is listening: every letter is kept for a person.
# Run it with the recorded answers beside it, and it sorts the post the same
# way every time, without asking any model:
#
#   python -m englishscript examples/triage.english --model replay:examples/triage_answers.txt
#
# Those answers were written for this example by hand. No model gave them.

A team is one of returns, billing or shipping.

For a team, returns means "the customer wants to send something back, or wants their money back".
For a team, billing means "the customer has a question about a charge, a price or an invoice".
For a team, shipping means "the customer is waiting for something that has not arrived".

A letter has a wording (text), a team (team, if any) and a summary (text, if any).
A letter can be urgent.
A letter can be held.

There is a letter called Ada whose wording is "The kettle I bought came with a crack in it. I would like my money back, please.".
There is a letter called Bo whose wording is "I was charged twice for my order last week. Please fix this today.".
There is a letter called Cy whose wording is "My parcel was meant to come on Monday and it still has not arrived.".
There is a letter called Di whose wording is "Do you sell gift cards?".

# A line saying what the letter wants, from a model that writes, or the start of
# the letter itself when no model is listening.
To work out the gist (text) of a note (text), ask the model "Say in one line what this customer wants.".

# Sort one letter. Each question is a judgement on its own, and the program
# combines them.
To sort a letter:
  Judge "Which team should handle this letter?" about the letter and let the pick (team) be the verdict and the sureness (percent) be the likelihood.
  Judge "Is the customer upset, or in a hurry?" about the letter and let the heat (percent) be the chance.
  # Sure enough to act: send it to the team.
  If the sureness is at least 85%:
    Set the letter's team to the pick.
  # Not sure: send it, but hold it for a person to check.
  Otherwise if the sureness is at least 50%:
    Set the letter's team to the pick.
    Now the letter is held.
  # Unlikely to be right: a person decides.
  Otherwise:
    Now the letter is held.
  If the heat is at least 70%:
    Now the letter is urgent.

For each letter:
  Try:
    Sort the letter.
    Set the letter's summary to the gist of the letter's wording.
  If that fails:
    Now the letter is held.
    If the problem is no model:
      Set the letter's summary to "(no model is listening)".
    Otherwise:
      Set the letter's summary to "(the model's answer could not be used)".

For each letter:
  Let the destination be "a person".
  If the letter has a team:
    Set the destination to "{the letter's team}".
  Let the flags be "".
  If the letter is urgent:
    Set the flags to " (urgent)".
  If the letter is held:
    Set the flags to "{the flags} (held for a person to check)".
  Let the line be the letter's summary or else "".
  Say "{the letter} to {the destination}{the flags}: {the line}"

Say "{the number of held letters} of {the number of letters} held for a person."
